JSONL Format
JSONL (JSON Lines, also called “newline-delimited JSON”) is a text format where each line is a complete, valid JSON object, separated by newlines.
Format Comparison
Regular JSON:
[
  {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-123&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Fix bug&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;open&quot;},
  {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-456&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Add feature&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;closed&quot;}
]
JSONL:
{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-123&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Fix bug&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;open&quot;}
{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-456&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Add feature&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;closed&quot;}
Why JSONL is Better for Some Use Cases
1. Streaming &amp; Append-Only
You can write new records without parsing the entire file:
echo &#039;{&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-789&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;New task&quot;}&#039; &gt;&gt; issues.jsonl
2. Line-by-Line Processing
Easy to parse incrementally without loading everything into memory:
# Process 1 million records without RAM explosion
cat huge.jsonl | while read line; do
  echo $line | jq &#039;.title&#039;
done
3. Git-Friendly
Merge conflicts are easier - each line is independent:
  {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-123&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Fix bug&quot;}
+ {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-456&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Your new task&quot;}
+ {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;bd-789&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;My new task&quot;}
vs. regular JSON where adding items causes bracket/comma conflicts.
4. Fault Tolerance
If one line is corrupted, the rest of the file is still valid. Regular JSON becomes completely unparseable.
Why Beads Uses JSONL
For Beads specifically, JSONL is perfect because:

Git merges - Multiple agents/branches can add issues without conflicts
Incremental updates - bd can append new issues without rewriting the entire file
Corruption resistance - A bad merge line doesn’t break the whole database
Fast parsing - Tools like bv can scan through thousands of issues efficiently

Reading JSONL
Most tools handle it easily:
# Command line
cat .beads/issues.jsonl | jq &#039;.id&#039;

# Python
with open(&#039;.beads/issues.jsonl&#039;) as f:
    for line in f:
        issue = json.loads(line)
        print(issue[&#039;title&#039;])

# JavaScript
const lines = fs.readFileSync(&#039;.beads/issues.jsonl&#039;, &#039;utf-8&#039;).split(&#039;\n&#039;);
lines.forEach(line =&gt; {
    if (line) {
        const issue = JSON.parse(line);
        console.log(issue.title);
    }
});
It’s essentially a simple database format that leverages text files’ natural line-based structure!
